                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "yew",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8e056fc2-cd44-3e2f-b22d-d437bd3ae7df",
                                        "entry_id": "2cc02f4c-9b1a-3ffa-984e-50eaf2ffe30c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A species of coniferous tree, Taxus baccata, with dark-green flat needle-like leaves and seeds bearing red arils, native to western, central and southern Europe, northwest Africa, northern Iran and southwest Asia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9a700d04-92fc-49fc-80d3-473073b2ee30",
                                                "sense_id": "8e056fc2-cd44-3e2f-b22d-d437bd3ae7df",
                                                "text": "Old Yew, which graspest at the stones\n⁠That name the under-lying dead,\n⁠Thy fibres net the dreamless head,\nThy roots are wrapt about the bones."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4317ece2-b89c-4f6b-be4f-809385158214",
                                                "sense_id": "8e056fc2-cd44-3e2f-b22d-d437bd3ae7df",
                                                "text": "Have a tree or two the witches particularly like, such as the alder, larch, cypress and hemlock; then, to counteract any possible evil effects, there must be a holly, yew, hazel, elder, mountain ash or juniper."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a6437ea5-c9ba-4cf5-b05f-2d2d1d0ef4cd",
                                                "sense_id": "8e056fc2-cd44-3e2f-b22d-d437bd3ae7df",
                                                "text": "At Darley Dale, in Derbyshire, there is another yew about 1,000 years younger with a 32 ft girth, while a mighty yew at Goudhurst, Kent, has a bench seating 12 in its hollow trunk."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
